{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789424925,"nonce":"xkIChFnG0PsrEDo7xUKR5cT3Y+k=","title":"Collector scanned a reached large block to twice its address","message":"`Marker::consider` pushed a large block onto the work list as (payload, end) while a slab block is pushed as (base, length), and `drain` reads the second element as a length. A large block reached during marking was therefore read from its payload to `2 * payload + total`, through whatever lies above it, until a guard or unmapped page faulted.\n\nReproducer: `bench_runner --runs 3 --no-cache --filter records,pipeline,tokenize` faulted every time in `collect` at a page-aligned address with `EXC_BAD_ACCESS code=2`; a kernel whose collection reaches a list buffer above the pooled size (records: a 600k-entry list) reads past it. Whether it faults depends on what is mapped above the block, so a standalone `zypy run` passed and the in-process suite crashed only in some layouts (ASLR); under lldb with ASLR off it never did.\n\nFound by recording the range each scan reads and printing it from a SIGBUS handler: the registry held the block as total 0x40010 and the scan range was payload..(2 * payload + 0x40000).\n\nThe same change makes two remaining unprobed reads report instead of fault: a free-list link that leaves the slabs stops the walk (the sweep rebuilds every list, so the rest is recovered as unreached), and a slab header the pool did not write is reported and the slab left alone.","files":null}]}